<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
options(width = 72, prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ")
@

\section{Introduction}

\pkg{rsimd} computes common operations on R's atomic vectors, such as sums,
elementwise arithmetic, predicates and elementary functions, with the single
instruction, multiple data (SIMD) instructions of x86-64 and arm64 CPUs.
Each function is a drop-in counterpart of a base R function, named
\code{simd\_} followed by what it computes, and follows base R's rules for
result types, missing values and warnings, with any differences documented
on its help page. The package detects the CPU's instruction sets when it is
loaded and uses the best implementation it supports; \code{simd\_current()}
names it and \code{simd\_available()} lists the alternatives, ending with the
plain C implementation \code{"none"}.

<<intro>>=
library(rsimd)
simd_current()
x <- c(1.5, 2.5, NA, 4)
simd_sum(x, na.rm = TRUE)
simd_add(x, 10)
@

\section{Complex numbers}

R stores a complex vector as pairs of doubles: the real part, then the
imaginary part. \pkg{rsimd} supports the complex operations that treat the
two parts separately:

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{simd\_add()}, \code{simd\_sub()} and \code{simd\_neg()};
\item \code{simd\_sum()}, in every \code{simd\_precision()} mode;
\item \code{simd\_conj()}, \code{simd\_re()} and \code{simd\_im()};
\item the predicates \code{simd\_is\_na()}, \code{simd\_is\_nan()},
  \code{simd\_is\_finite()} and \code{simd\_is\_infinite()}, with their
  \code{\_any} and \code{\_all} forms, and \code{simd\_any\_na()},
  \code{simd\_count\_na()} and \code{simd\_which\_na()}.
\end{itemize}

A double, integer or logical operand of \code{simd\_add()} or
\code{simd\_sub()} is converted to complex, and a length-one operand is
reused for every element, as in base R:

<<complex-arith>>=
z <- complex(real = c(1, -2, 3), imaginary = c(0.5, 1, -1))
simd_add(z, 1i)
simd_sub(c(10, 20, 30), z)
simd_conj(z)
simd_re(z)
simd_sum(z)
@

Missing values propagate part by part, so a sum can have a missing real part
and a known imaginary part, exactly as with \code{sum()}. A complex number
is missing for \code{is.na()} when either part is, so \code{na.rm = TRUE}
removes the whole element:

<<complex-na>>=
w <- c(1 + 2i, NA, 3 + 1i)
simd_im(w)
simd_sum(w)
Im(simd_sum(w))
simd_sum(w, na.rm = TRUE)
simd_is_na(complex(real = 1, imaginary = NaN))
@

The other functions reject complex input with an error, including
multiplication and division, \code{Mod()}, \code{Arg()}, comparisons and the
elementary functions. These need the two parts of each number to interact,
and are planned for a later version.

<<complex-unsupported>>=
tryCatch(simd_mul(z, z), error = conditionMessage)
@

